%S A400686 1,0,1,10,72,472,2962,18212,110893,672457,4073294,24686093,149813411,
%T A400686 910781878,5547624104,33855603459,206992108147,1267737199514,
%U A400686 7776835927485,47777124310830,293918980584547,1810408453078074,11164048839580375,68916351740944308,425835116630334241
%N A400686 a(n) is the number of ways to divide a 2*n X 2*n square into two congruent connected parts, each having the same perimeter as the square, by a simple lattice path, up to the symmetries of the square.
%C A400686 A cut is a simple path of unit lattice edges with two distinct endpoints on the boundary and no other boundary points. If its length is p and the boundary arcs between its endpoints have lengths s and 8*n - s, the two region perimeters are p + s and p + 8*n - s. Both equal 8*n, so p = s = 4*n.
%C A400686 By a result of Sadovnichii, a cut into two congruent parts is invariant under the half-turn about the center, which exchanges the parts. The half-turn swaps the endpoints, so the midpoint of the path is the center. Hence each half of the path runs from a boundary endpoint to the center, has length 2*n, and determines the other half.
%C A400686 Up to symmetries of the square, an endpoint can be placed at (2*k, 0) with 1 <= k <= floor(n/2); odd offsets are excluded by parity, and k = 0 is impossible because a cut cannot follow the boundary. For k = 1 (endpoints two grid units from the nearest corner), the number of cuts is A000108(n-2)*(2*n - 1)*(2*n - 3)*(n^2 - n + 2)/(n*(n + 1)) ~ 4^(n - 1)*sqrt(n/Pi) for n >= 3. This gives the first lower bound in the formula section.
%C A400686 Consider cuts whose half-path from the top side to the center has no downward steps. Such a path has n upward and n horizontal steps and is encoded by a vector u = (u(1), ..., u(n)) with sum |u(i)| = n and entries of both signs, where u(i) gives the horizontal displacement on row i. Every such vector gives a valid cut, and exactly u and -u represent the same symmetry class. Counting these vectors gives the second lower bound in the formula section. Their number of symmetry classes is asymptotic to (3 + 2*sqrt(2))^n/(2^(7/4)*sqrt(Pi*n)), yielding the stated lower bound on the exponential growth rate.
%H A400686 Felix Huber, <a href="/A400686/a400686.pdf">Illustration of a(3) = 10</a>.
%H A400686 Felix Huber, <a href="/A400686/a400686_1.txt">Maple program</a>.
%H A400686 A. Yu. Sadovnichii, <a href="https://www.mathnet.ru/eng/mzm14526">Problem of cutting a polygon into two congruent parts</a>, Mat. Zametki 117:3 (2025), 443-452.
%F A400686 a(n) >= A000108(n-2)*(2*n - 1)*(2*n - 3)*(n^2 - n + 2)/(n*(n + 1)), n >= 3.
%F A400686 a(n) >= (A001850(n) + A001850(n-1) - 2*binomial(2*n, n))/4, n >= 1.
%F A400686 liminf_{n->infinity} a(n)^(1/n) >= 3 + 2*sqrt(2).
%F A400686 a(n) <= A400685(n).
%e A400686 a(0) = 1: the empty cut is counted.
%e A400686 a(3) = 10: the square is 6 X 6 with perimeter 24. Up to symmetries of the square, there are 10 simple cuts that divide it into two congruent connected parts, each of perimeter 24 (see the linked illustration).
%p A400686 # See Huber link.
